    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, '1: In a standard Posteroanterior (PA) chest X-ray, the X-ray beam travels from:', '["Front to back", "Back to front", "Side to side", "Top to bottom"]', 1, 'In a PA view, the patient stands with their chest against the detector, and the beam enters from the posterior (back) side.'),
    (v_topic_id, '2: Why is the PA view preferred over the Anteroposterior (AP) view for evaluating heart size?', '["It is easier for the patient", "It minimizes magnification of the heart", "It shows the ribs better", "It requires less radiation"]', 1, 'Because the heart is an anterior structure, placing it closer to the detector (as in a PA view) minimizes divergent beam magnification, providing a more accurate size.'),
    (v_topic_id, '3: Which of the following is the most "radiopaque" (whitest) on a standard X-ray?', '["Air", "Fat", "Soft tissue/Water", "Metal"]', 3, 'Metal absorbs the most X-rays, appearing bright white. The order of increasing density is Air < Fat < Soft Tissue < Bone < Metal.'),
    (v_topic_id, '4: On a chest X-ray, "Air" appears:', '["White", "Grey", "Black", "Translucent"]', 2, 'Air is radiolucent (it allows X-rays to pass through easily), so it appears black on the film.'),
    (v_topic_id, '5: To assess if a chest X-ray has adequate "Inspiration," how many posterior ribs should be visible above the diaphragm?', '["5 to 6", "7 to 8", "9 to 10", "12 to 13"]', 2, 'A good inspiratory effort is indicated by the 9th or 10th posterior rib (or 6th anterior rib) intersecting the diaphragm at the midclavicular line.'),
    (v_topic_id, '6: "Rotation" on a chest X-ray is assessed by measuring the distance between:', '["The clavicles and the sternum", "The medial ends of the clavicles and the spinous processes of the vertebrae", "The ribs and the scapula", "The trachea and the hila"]', 1, 'The medial ends of the clavicles should be equidistant from the vertebral spinous processes.'),
    (v_topic_id, '7: If a patient is rotated to the RIGHT, the medial end of the RIGHT clavicle will appear:', '["Closer to the spinous process", "Further from the spinous process", "Higher than the left clavicle", "Lower than the left clavicle"]', 1, 'Rotation shifts the spinous processes away from the side the patient is turned toward.'),
    (v_topic_id, '8: An "Over-penetrated" chest X-ray (too much radiation) will look:', '["Too white", "Too dark (black)", "Blurry", "Small"]', 1, 'Excessive penetration makes the image too dark, making it difficult to see subtle lung markings.'),
    (v_topic_id, '9: An "Under-penetrated" chest X-ray will look:', '["Too white", "Too dark", "Too small", "Distorted"]', 0, 'Inadequate penetration makes the image too white, potentially obscuring retrocardiac structures and the spine.'),
    (v_topic_id, '10: On a technically adequate PA film, you should just be able to see the _____ through the heart shadow.', '["Esophagus", "Thoracic vertebrae", "Aorta", "Pulmonary veins"]', 1, 'The lower thoracic vertebrae should be faintly visible behind the cardiac silhouette.'),
    (v_topic_id, '11: The "ABCDE" systematic approach to reading a CXR stands for:', '["Airway, Bones, Circulation, Diaphragm, Everything else", "Airway, Breathing, Cardiac, Diaphragm, Extras (Effusions, Edema)", "Atria, Bronchi, Clavicles, Density, Edema", "Airway, Bones, Cardiac, Diaphragm, Extras (Tubes, Lines, Scars)"]', 3, 'This mnemonic ensures that all components of the film are examined systematically.'),
    (v_topic_id, '12: The "Hila" of the lungs consist primarily of:', '["Lymph nodes only", "Pulmonary arteries, veins, and major bronchi", "The esophagus and aorta", "The thymus"]', 1, 'The hila are the "roots" of the lungs where major vessels and airways enter/exit.'),
    (v_topic_id, '13: Which hilum is normally positioned slightly higher than the other?', '["Right hilum", "Left hilum", "They are always at the same level", "It depends on the patient''s age"]', 1, 'The left hilum is higher than the right in ~70% of individuals, or they are at the same level. The right hilum should NEVER be higher than the left.'),
    (v_topic_id, '14: The "Silhouette Sign" occurs when:', '["A shadow is cast on the ribs", "Two structures of different density are adjacent", "Two structures of the same density are in contact, causing their shared border to disappear", "The heart looks larger than normal"]', 2, 'This is a fundamental sign in radiology used to localize opacities (e.g., a right middle lobe pneumonia obscuring the right heart border).'),
    (v_topic_id, '15: Loss of the RIGHT HEART BORDER silhouette suggests an opacity in the:', '["Right Upper Lobe", "Right Middle Lobe", "Right Lower Lobe", "Posterior Mediastinum"]', 1, 'The right middle lobe is anatomically adjacent to the right atrium.'),
    (v_topic_id, '16: Loss of the LEFT HEART BORDER (lingula) silhouette suggests an opacity in the:', '["Left Upper Lobe (Lingula segment)", "Left Lower Lobe", "Aortic Arch", "Diaphragm"]', 0, 'The lingula segment of the left upper lobe is adjacent to the left ventricle.'),
    (v_topic_id, '17: Loss of the DIAPHRAGM silhouette suggests an opacity in the:', '["Upper lobes", "Middle lobe", "Lower lobes", "Pleura"]', 2, 'The lower lobes of both lungs rest on the diaphragm.'),
    (v_topic_id, '18: Loss of the AORTIC KNUCKLE silhouette suggests an opacity in the:', '["Left Upper Lobe (Posterior segment)", "Left Lower Lobe", "Right Upper Lobe", "Anterior Mediastinum"]', 0, 'The aortic arch is a posterior structure in the superior mediastinum, adjacent to the upper lobe.'),
    (v_topic_id, '19: An "Air Bronchogram" is defined as:', '["Air-filled bronchi visible within an area of lung consolidation", "Air in the pleural space", "A dilated bronchus", "A tumor in the airway"]', 0, 'Consolidated lung (fluid/pus/blood) provides a dense background that makes the air-filled bronchi visible.'),
    (v_topic_id, '20: The presence of an air bronchogram indicates that the pathology is located in the:', '["Pleural space", "Mediastinum", "Lung parenchyma (alveoli)", "Chest wall"]', 2, 'Air bronchograms prove that the abnormality is alveolar (consolidation), not just a pleural effusion or a solid mass blocking the airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, '21: What is the "Cardiothoracic Ratio" (CTR) and what is its normal limit in an adult PA film?', '["Width of heart / Width of thorax; normal is < 0.33", "Width of heart / Width of thorax; normal is < 0.50", "Width of thorax / Width of heart; normal is < 2.0", "Area of heart / Area of lungs; normal is < 0.25"]', 1, 'A CTR > 50% on a PA film suggests cardiomegaly (though AP films and poor inspiration can give false positives).'),
    (v_topic_id, '22: On a LATERAL chest X-ray, the space behind the heart and in front of the spine is called the:', '["Retrosternal space", "Retrocardiac space", "Aortopulmonary window", "Costophrenic angle"]', 1, 'This space should normally be radiolucent. Opacification here suggests lower lobe pathology.'),
    (v_topic_id, '23: On a LATERAL CXR, the spine should appear to get _____ as you move from top to bottom.', '["Whiter (more dense)", "Darker (more radiolucent)", "Wider", "Narrower"]', 1, 'The "Spine Sign" refers to the loss of this normal darkening, indicating a lower lobe opacity.'),
    (v_topic_id, '24: The "Aortopulmonary Window" is located between the:', '["Aorta and the spine", "Aortic arch and the left pulmonary artery", "Trachea and the esophagus", "Right and left hila"]', 1, 'This is a common site for lymphadenopathy (e.g., in lung cancer or sarcoidosis).'),
    (v_topic_id, '25: In a healthy person, the "Right Hemidiaphragm" is usually _____ than the left.', '["1 to 2 cm higher", "1 to 2 cm lower", "At exactly the same level", "More horizontal"]', 0, 'The liver pushes the right hemidiaphragm slightly upward.'),
    (v_topic_id, '26: Which of the following can cause the LEFT hemidiaphragm to be higher than the right?', '["Normal variant", "Splenomegaly", "Gastric distension", "Left lower lobe collapse (atelectasis)"]', 3, 'Loss of lung volume (atelectasis) pulls the diaphragm upward.'),
    (v_topic_id, '27: "Kerley B lines" are short, horizontal lines at the lung bases near the pleura. They indicate:', '["Pneumonia", "Interlobular septal thickening (e.g., due to pulmonary edema)", "Rib fractures", "Bronchiectasis"]', 1, 'Kerley B lines are a classic sign of interstitial pulmonary edema.'),
    (v_topic_id, '28: "Cephalization" (or "Antler sign") on a CXR refers to:', '["Dilation of the upper lobe pulmonary vessels", "Calcification of the aorta", "A tumor in the apex", "Widening of the mediastinum"]', 0, 'This occurs in pulmonary venous hypertension (e.g., heart failure) as blood is shunted to the upper lobes.'),
    (v_topic_id, '29: The "Bat-wing" or "Butterfly" pattern of perihilar opacities is classic for:', '["Acute pulmonary edema (alveolar)", "Tuberculosis", "Aspiration pneumonia", "Lung cancer"]', 0, 'This represents rapid accumulation of fluid in the alveoli in acute LV failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, '30: A "Pneumothorax" is characterized by:', '["Increased lung markings", "A visible visceral pleural line with an absence of lung markings peripheral to it", "A dense white opacity", "A shifting mediastinum toward the affected side"]', 1, 'The lung collapses away from the chest wall, leaving a radiolucent (black) void without lung vessels.'),
    (v_topic_id, '31: A "Tension Pneumothorax" is a medical emergency. What is the key RADIOGRAPHIC sign that distinguishes it from a simple pneumothorax?', '["The size of the pneumothorax", "Flattening of the diaphragm", "Shift of the mediastinum AWAY from the side of the pneumothorax", "Multiple rib fractures"]', 2, 'The pressure buildup pushes the heart and trachea toward the opposite side, compromising venous return.'),
    (v_topic_id, '32: "Pleural Effusion" typically presents as:', '["Blunting of the costophrenic angles", "Increased air in the lungs", "A thin black line", "Dilation of the hila"]', 0, 'Fluid pools in the most dependent parts of the pleural space.'),
    (v_topic_id, '33: The "Meniscus Sign" describes:', '["The appearance of a rib fracture", "The concave upward upper border of a pleural effusion", "A hole in the lung", "The shape of the heart in pericardial effusion"]', 1, 'Fluid "climbs" the chest wall due to surface tension and negative pleural pressure.'),
    (v_topic_id, '34: To detect a very small pleural effusion (too small to see on an upright film), which view is most sensitive?', '["Supine AP", "Lateral decubitus (affected side down)", "Lateral decubitus (affected side up)", "Lordotic view"]', 1, 'Gravity causes even small amounts of fluid to layer out along the dependent chest wall.'),
    (v_topic_id, '35: "Atelectasis" refers to:', '["Over-inflation of the lung", "Collapse or loss of volume of a portion of the lung", "Infection of the lung", "Fluid in the alveoli"]', 1, 'Atelectasis is characterized by displacement of fissures and shifting of structures TOWARD the collapse.'),
    (v_topic_id, '36: What is the most common cause of "Plate-like" (discoid) atelectasis?', '["Tumor", "Poor inspiratory effort (e.g., due to pain or post-op)", "Heart failure", "Tuberculosis"]', 1, 'This is commonly seen at the lung bases in post-operative patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, '37: A "Lobar Collapse" (e.g., Right Upper Lobe) will cause the hila and mediastinum to shift:', '["Toward the side of the collapse", "Away from the side of the collapse", "It does not cause a shift", "Downward"]', 0, 'Volume loss "pulls" the mobile mediastinal structures.'),
    (v_topic_id, '38: The "Golden S-sign" is a specific appearance of Right Upper Lobe collapse that suggests:', '["Pneumonia", "An underlying mass (e.g., bronchogenic carcinoma) obstructing the airway", "Pleural effusion", "Atelectasis due to a mucus plug"]', 1, 'The minor fissure is pulled up, but is held down in the center by the obstructing mass, creating an "S" shape.'),
    (v_topic_id, '39: On a frontal CXR, the "Minor Fissure" (horizontal fissure) separates the:', '["Right upper lobe from the right middle lobe", "Right middle lobe from the right lower lobe", "Left upper lobe from the left lower lobe", "Right lung from the left lung"]', 0, 'The minor fissure is usually visible at the level of the 4th rib.'),
    (v_topic_id, '40: The "Major Fissures" (oblique fissures) are best seen on which view?', '["PA", "Lateral", "Lordotic", "Decubitus"]', 1, 'They run obliquely from the level of the T4/T5 vertebrae to the anterior diaphragm.'),
    (v_topic_id, '41: A "Lung Nodule" is defined as a discrete pulmonary opacity measuring:', '["< 1 cm", "< 3 cm", "> 3 cm", "> 5 cm"]', 1, 'Opacities > 3 cm are termed "Lung Masses" and have a higher probability of malignancy.'),
    (v_topic_id, '42: Which of the following features of a lung nodule is most suggestive of malignancy?', '["Smooth, well-defined borders", "Spiculated (star-burst) or irregular borders", "Diffuse calcification", "Stability for more than 2 years"]', 1, 'Irregular borders reflect invasive growth into the surrounding parenchyma.'),
    (v_topic_id, '43: "Popcorn calcification" within a pulmonary nodule is characteristic of a:', '["Bronchogenic carcinoma", "Hamartoma (benign)", "Granuloma", "Metastatic lesion"]', 1, 'This is a classic "benign" calcification pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, '44: "Honeycombing" (multiple small cystic airspaces with thick walls) is the end-stage radiographic appearance of:', '["Emphysema", "Interstitial Lung Disease (Pulmonary Fibrosis)", "Congestive Heart Failure", "Acute Bronchitis"]', 1, 'This represents irreversible destruction of the lung architecture.'),
    (v_topic_id, '45: "Ground-glass opacity" (GGO) refers to:', '["A dense white mass", "Hazy opacification that does not obscure underlying bronchovascular markings", "A black hole in the lung", "Fluid in the pleural space"]', 1, 'GGO is a non-specific sign of partial filling of alveoli or thickening of the interstitium.'),
    (v_topic_id, '46: "Consolidation" is distinguished from ground-glass opacity because it:', '["Is blacker", "Obscures the underlying vessels and airway walls", "Is always localized", "Has no air bronchograms"]', 1, 'Consolidation involves total replacement of alveolar air with fluid, cells, or pus.'),
    (v_topic_id, '47: "Emphysema" is characterized on CXR by:', '["Hyperinflation, flattened diaphragms, and increased retrosternal clear space", "Lung collapse", "Increased lung markings", "Pleural thickening"]', 0, 'Destruction of alveoli leads to air trapping and lung expansion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '48: A "Pneumoperitoneum" (free air under the diaphragm) is best detected on which view?', '["Supine Abdominal X-ray", "Upright Chest X-ray", "Lateral Decubitus Chest X-ray", "Prone Abdominal X-ray"]', 1, 'Air rises to the highest point; an upright CXR is extremely sensitive for even small amounts of subdiaphragmatic air.'),
    (v_topic_id, '49: The presence of "Pneumoperitoneum" usually indicates:', '["Gallstones", "Perforation of a hollow viscus (e.g., perforated ulcer)", "Intestinal obstruction", "Liver cirrhosis"]', 1, 'This is a surgical emergency.'),
    (v_topic_id, '50: "Mediastinal Widening" (> 8 cm on a PA film) is a concerning sign for:', '["Aortic dissection", "Mediastinal hematoma", "Lymphadenopathy", "All of the above"]', 3, 'In the context of trauma or tearing chest pain, aortic injury must be ruled out.'),
    (v_topic_id, '51: Which structure forms the RIGHT border of the mediastinum on a frontal CXR?', '["Right Ventricle", "Superior Vena Cava and Right Atrium", "Right Hilum", "Aorta"]', 1, 'The Right Atrium forms the bulk of the right heart border.'),
    (v_topic_id, '52: Which structure forms the LEFT border of the mediastinum?', '["Left Atrium", "Aortic arch, Pulmonary artery, and Left Ventricle", "Left Hilum", "Superior Vena Cava"]', 1, 'The "aortic knob" and left ventricular shadow dominate the left border.'),
    (v_topic_id, '53: The "Carina" (the bifurcation of the trachea) should have an angle of less than:', '["45 degrees", "60 degrees", "90 degrees", "120 degrees"]', 2, 'A widened carinal angle can suggest left atrial enlargement (pushing the bronchi apart).'),
    (v_topic_id, '54: An "Endotracheal (ET) tube" tip should ideally be positioned:', '["In the right mainstem bronchus", "3 to 5 cm above the carina", "At the level of the vocal cords", "In the esophagus"]', 1, 'This prevents accidental extubation or selective intubation of one bronchus (usually the right).'),
    (v_topic_id, '55: A "Nasogastric (NG) tube" is correctly positioned when the tip is:', '["In the distal esophagus", "Below the diaphragm, ideally in the gastric body/antrum", "In the right bronchus", "Above the clavicles"]', 1, 'The tube must cross the diaphragm and the "side-hole" must be within the stomach.'),
    (v_topic_id, '56: A "Central Venous Catheter" (CVC) tip should ideally be located in the:', '["Right Atrium", "Superior Vena Cava (SVC) or SVC-atrial junction", "Subclavian vein", "Jugular vein"]', 1, 'Placing it too deep (in the atrium) can cause arrhythmias or perforation.'),
    (v_topic_id, '57: "Deep Sulcus Sign" on a SUPINE chest X-ray is a sign of:', '["Pleural effusion", "Pneumothorax", "Diaphragmatic hernia", "Pneumonia"]', 1, 'In a supine patient, air in the pleura collects anteriorly and inferiorly, deepening the costophrenic angle.'),
    (v_topic_id, '58: "Sail Sign" in a PEDIATRIC chest X-ray is a normal appearance of the:', '["Heart", "Thymus", "Liver", "Spleen"]', 1, 'The thymus is large in infants and can look like a "sail" in the superior mediastinum.'),
    (v_topic_id, '59: "Cavitary lesions" (air-filled spaces with a thick wall) are classically seen in:', '["Post-primary Tuberculosis", "Lung abscess", "Squamous cell carcinoma", "All of the above"]', 3, 'Cavitation implies tissue necrosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '60: "Water-bottle heart" (a very large, globular cardiac silhouette) is characteristic of:', '["Heart failure", "Massive pericardial effusion", "Mitral stenosis", "Aortic aneurysm"]', 1, 'The heart takes on this shape when there is significant fluid in the pericardium.'),
    (v_topic_id, '61: "Egg-on-a-string" heart is a classic radiographic description for:', '["Tetralogy of Fallot", "Transposition of the Great Arteries", "Total anomalous pulmonary venous return", "Tricuspid atresia"]', 1, 'This is due to a narrow superior mediastinum and an enlarged globular heart.'),
    (v_topic_id, '62: "Boot-shaped heart" (C\u0153ur en sabot) is classic for:', '["Transposition of the Great Arteries", "Tetralogy of Fallot", "Atrial Septal Defect", "Coarctation of the Aorta"]', 1, 'The "toe" of the boot is the upturned apex due to right ventricular hypertrophy.'),
    (v_topic_id, '63: "Rib notching" (erosions on the inferior surface of the ribs) is a classic sign of:', '["Rib fractures", "Coarctation of the aorta", "Multiple myeloma", "Osteoporosis"]', 1, 'This is caused by dilated intercostal arteries acting as collaterals.'),
    (v_topic_id, '64: "Hampton''s Hump" is a wedge-shaped opacity at the lung periphery. It is a sign of:', '["Lung cancer", "Pulmonary infarction (due to PE)", "Pneumonia", "Tuberculosis"]', 1, 'This is a rare but classic sign of pulmonary embolism.'),
    (v_topic_id, '65: "Westermark Sign" is a focal area of oligemia (decreased vascular markings) distal to a large PE. This indicates:', '["Consolidation", "Decreased blood flow due to an obstructing clot", "Atelectasis", "Pleural effusion"]', 1, 'Like Hampton''s Hump, this is a classic but insensitive sign for PE.'),
    (v_topic_id, '66: A "Pleural plaque" (calcified or non-calcified) along the chest wall or diaphragm is strongly associated with:', '["Smoking", "Asbestos exposure", "Tuberculosis", "Coal dust inhalation"]', 1, 'Asbestos-related plaques are often most visible on the diaphragmatic pleura.'),
    (v_topic_id, '67: "Eggshell calcification" of the hilar lymph nodes is a hallmark of:', '["Sarcoidosis", "Silicosis", "Tuberculosis", "Lung cancer"]', 1, 'This is a specific pattern seen in coal workers or those exposed to silica dust.'),
    (v_topic_id, '68: "Stage 1 Sarcoidosis" is characterized by:', '["Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy (BHL)", "BHL + lung infiltrates", "Lung infiltrates only", "Pulmonary fibrosis"]', 0, 'Stage 1 involves lymph nodes only; Stage 4 is end-stage fibrosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '69: The "Panda Sign" and "Gallium-67 scan" are sometimes mentioned in relation to:', '["Tuberculosis", "Sarcoidosis", "Lymphoma", "Pneumonia"]', 1, 'These are older nuclear medicine tests for sarcoidosis involvement.'),
    (v_topic_id, '70: "Miliary" pattern consists of millions of 1-3 mm tiny nodules scattered throughout both lungs. This is classic for:', '["Miliary Tuberculosis", "Metastatic thyroid cancer", "Silicosis", "All of the above"]', 3, 'While TB is the most common association, other conditions can mimic it.'),
    (v_topic_id, '71: "Snowstorm" appearance of the lungs can be a sign of:', '["Fat embolism", "Metastatic choriocarcinoma", "Severe pulmonary edema", "Both A and B"]', 3, 'This refers to diffuse, small nodular opacities.'),
    (v_topic_id, '72: On an Abdominal X-ray, "Rigler''s Sign" (double wall sign) is seen when:', '["There is air on both the inside and outside of the bowel wall", "The bowel is obstructed", "There is fluid in the abdomen", "The patient has gallstones"]', 0, 'Rigler''s sign is diagnostic for pneumoperitoneum on a supine film.'),
    (v_topic_id, '73: "Football Sign" on an abdominal film refers to:', '["A large bubble of air outlining the entire peritoneal cavity", "A single dilated loop of bowel", "A calcified gallstone", "Ascites"]', 0, 'This is another sign of massive pneumoperitoneum, usually in infants.'),
    (v_topic_id, '74: "Coffee-bean sign" on an abdominal X-ray is classic for:', '["Cecal volvulus", "Sigmoid volvulus", "Small bowel obstruction", "Intussusception"]', 1, 'The twisted loop of sigmoid colon looks like a giant coffee bean.'),
    (v_topic_id, '75: "Lead-pipe colon" (loss of haustral markings) is a radiographic feature of:', '["Crohn''s disease", "Ulcerative colitis (Chronic)", "Colon cancer", "Irritable bowel syndrome"]', 1, 'Chronic inflammation lead to a smooth, tubular appearance of the colon.'),
    (v_topic_id, '76: "Thumbprinting" on a bowel film suggests:', '["Bowel wall edema (e.g., from ischemia or severe colitis)", "Constipation", "Polyps", "Perforation"]', 0, 'The thickened haustra look like thumbprints pressing into the air-filled lumen.'),
    (v_topic_id, '77: The "3-6-9 Rule" for bowel diameter limits refers to:', '["Small bowel < 3cm, Large bowel < 6cm, Cecum < 9cm", "Small bowel < 6cm, Large bowel < 3cm, Cecum < 9cm", "Small bowel < 3cm, Large bowel < 9cm, Cecum < 6cm", "Esophagus < 3cm, Small bowel < 6cm, Stomach < 9cm"]', 0, 'Diameters exceeding these limits suggest obstruction or ileus.'),
    (v_topic_id, '78: "String sign" (a very narrow lumen) on a barium study of the terminal ileum is classic for:', '["Ulcerative colitis", "Crohn''s disease", "Appendicitis", "Celiac disease"]', 1, 'This is due to severe transmural inflammation and spasm/stricture.'),
    (v_topic_id, '79: "Apple-core lesion" (annular constriction) on a barium enema is highly suspicious for:', '["Colorectal carcinoma", "Diverticulitis", "Volvulus", "Intussusception"]', 0, 'The circumferential growth of a tumor narrows the lumen.'),
    (v_topic_id, '80: "Bird''s beak" appearance on an esophagram is the hallmark of:', '["Esophageal cancer", "Achalasia", "Gastroesophageal reflux", "Zenker''s diverticulum"]', 1, 'The failure of the LES to relax results in a tapered narrowing.'),
    (v_topic_id, '81: "Corkscrew esophagus" is diagnostic for:', '["Achalasia", "Diffuse esophageal spasm", "Esophageal varices", "Esophagitis"]', 1, 'Multiple simultaneous, non-peristaltic contractions give this appearance.'),
    (v_topic_id, '82: On a skull X-ray, "Salt and pepper" appearance of the calvarium is seen in:', '["Multiple myeloma", "Hyperparathyroidism (renal osteodystrophy)", "Osteoporosis", "Paget''s disease"]', 1, 'Multiple tiny lucencies are caused by bone resorption.'),
    (v_topic_id, '83: "Punch-out" lytic lesions on a skull X-ray are classic for:', '["Multiple myeloma", "Metastatic breast cancer", "Brain tumor", "Stroke"]', 0, 'Unlike the hazy salt-and-pepper look, myeloma causes discrete, sharply defined holes.'),
    (v_topic_id, '84: On a hand X-ray, "Rat-bite" erosions (periarticular erosions) are a sign of:', '["Rheumatoid arthritis", "Gout", "Osteoarthritis", "Septic arthritis"]', 1, 'Gouty tophi cause "punched-out" erosions with overhanging edges.'),
    (v_topic_id, '85: "Bamboo spine" (fusion of the vertebrae) is the radiographic hallmark of:', '["Rheumatoid arthritis", "Ankylosing spondylitis", "Osteoporosis", "Disc herniation"]', 1, 'Syndesmophytes bridge the vertebral bodies, creating a solid column.'),
    (v_topic_id, '86: A "Sunburst" periosteal reaction or "Codman''s triangle" in a long bone of a child/teenager suggests:', '["Osteosarcoma", "Ewing sarcoma", "Osteomyelitis", "Bone cyst"]', 0, 'These are aggressive patterns of periosteal response to an underlying malignant tumor.'),
    (v_topic_id, '87: "Onion-skin" periosteal reaction is more characteristic of:', '["Osteosarcoma", "Ewing sarcoma", "Osteoid osteoma", "Greenstick fracture"]', 1, 'Ewing sarcoma often shows this laminated appearance.'),
    (v_topic_id, '88: A "Dinner fork deformity" describes which type of fracture?', '["Smith fracture", "Colles fracture", "Scaphoid fracture", "Boxer fracture"]', 1, 'Colles fracture involves distal radius fracture with dorsal displacement/angulation.'),
    (v_topic_id, '89: "Scaphoid fractures" are notorious for causing _____ if not treated properly.', '["Nerve damage", "Avascular necrosis (AVN) of the proximal pole", "Muscle wasting", "Skin infections"]', 1, 'The scaphoid has a retrograde blood supply.'),
    (v_topic_id, '90: "Fat pad sign" (or Sail sign) at the elbow indicates:', '["An occult fracture causing a joint effusion", "Dislocation", "Nerve entrapment", "Bursitis"]', 0, 'Fluid in the joint space displaces the normal fat pads away from the bone.'),
    (v_topic_id, '91: "Scottie Dog" sign with a broken neck on an oblique lumbar spine film indicates:', '["Spondylolisthesis", "Spondylolysis (pars interarticularis defect)", "Spinal stenosis", "Disc herniation"]', 1, 'The "neck" of the dog is the pars interarticularis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '92: In a "Hanging drop" fracture of the orbit, the most common symptom is:', '["Blindness", "Diplopia (double vision) on upward gaze", "Hearing loss", "Headache"]', 1, 'Entrapment of the inferior rectus muscle in the orbital floor fracture limits eye movement.'),
    (v_topic_id, '93: "Thumb sign" on a lateral neck X-ray is classic for:', '["Croup", "Epiglottitis", "Retropharyngeal abscess", "Foreign body"]', 1, 'The swollen epiglottis looks like a thumb. This is a pediatric emergency.'),
    (v_topic_id, '94: "Steeple sign" (subglottic narrowing) on a neck X-ray is classic for:', '["Epiglottitis", "Croup (Laryngotracheobronchitis)", "Asthma", "Tonsillitis"]', 1, 'The trachea tapers toward the top like a church steeple.'),
    (v_topic_id, '95: In a patient with "Pleural Effusion," why do we say the fluid "layers out" on a decubitus film?', '["Because fluid is denser than air and follows gravity", "Because the patient is moving", "Because of the X-ray angle", "Because of lung collapse"]', 0, 'This confirms the fluid is "free-flowing" and not loculated (trapped by adhesions).'),
